      "content": "To distinguish between pit-fired and kiln-fired techniques, examine the cross-sections of the shards. Pit-fired ceramics typically display a surface layer of unburnt, crystalline, crystallized salt (i.e., natron or halite), which may be mixed with other minerals, and may lack a distinctly glassy or matte finish. In contrast, kiln-fired ceramics often display a more consistent, glassy, or matte finish throughout the surface. The presence of sand or ash particles can also be indicative of kiln firing, as they tend to accumulate on the surface.",
